    "text color" : "[0.0119935916736722, 0.30600443482398987, 0.6350042223930359]",
    "content" : "ROBERTBAYNHAM EXMOUTH, DEVON"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 42,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 533.378, 577.63, 602.721, 597.157 ],
    "font" : "Stag-Bold",
    "font size" : 8.99,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "Stokingthefires ofdebate"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 43,
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 533.378, 496.776, 602.847, 575.296 ],
    "font" : "StRydeRegular",
    "font size" : 6.368,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "I was interested to read Alan MacDonald’s good humoured take on Stoke-on Trent’s location (Your View, 3 January). However confusion still reigns. He refers to Stoke-on-Trent as being undoubtedly the capital of the Potteries. Stokeon-Trent is the Potteries"
  }, {
    "type" : "paragraph",
    "pdfua_tag" : "P",
    "id" : 44,
    "level" : "1",
    "page number" : 1,
    "bounding box" : [ 533.378, 432.7, 598.52, 496.98 ],
    "font" : "StRydeRegular",
    "font size" : 6.368,
    "text color" : "[0.13725490868091583, 0.12156862765550613, 0.125490203499794]",
    "content" : "– a city of six towns of which Stoke is only one. I guess one could argue that the town of Stoke is the capital – but most people who live there give primacy to Hanley. PHILMANN HARROW, MIDDLESEX"
  }, {
